# ThreadSanitizer suppressions: live reads that are races by design.
# Use: TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$PWD/tools/sanitize/tsan.supp" (see docs/testing/sanitizers.md, section 8).
#
# race_top matches the top frame of an access, the code that touches the memory itself. A plain race:
# entry matches any frame of either stack, and every emulation-thread access has Core::CPUFrameCycle or
# MainLoop::Run below it: such an entry would hide real races too. The one exception is anchored on a
# global object (^...$), which race: also matches.
#
# Allowed here: a read of a byte or an aligned scalar whose worst case is a value one step older or newer.
# Not allowed: containers, pointers to storage that can be reallocated or freed, read-modify-write.
# Those are real bugs (BreakpointManager, the shared memory migration).

# A. Tools read guest memory live (debugger views, WebAPI, Lua/Python, tests) while the CPU writes it.
# The buffer is never freed while the instance lives (a shared memory migration retires the old one),
# so a read sees the byte before or after the write
race_top:Memory::DirectReadFromZ80Memory

# C. Tests read the machine state live (frame counter, registers, flags) to wait for it or check it.
# The top frame is in the test source itself: production code never matches
race_top:_test.cpp
race_top:testwaithelper.h

# The machine time of a coordinator operation log entry (frame, t-state): an operation that does not hold
# the CPU is stamped while it runs. The entry is shown by the status calls only
race_top:CoordinatorPorts::Now

# D. Feature flag caches: a component copies a feature's bool when it changes (on the thread that changed it), the
# emulation thread reads its copy per instruction or per draw. The worst case is one draw or step with the old value.
# Pointers are not here: Core::SelectMemoryInterface swaps the memory interface and stays reported
race_top:Screen::UpdateFeatureCache
race_top:Z80::UpdateFeatureCache
race_top:FeatureManager::onFeatureChanged

# Not a race: concurrent output to the synchronized standard streams is allowed ([iostream.objects]);
# TSan sees libc++ resetting the field width inside the stream. Anchored on the global, not on a function
race:^std::__1::cout$
race:^std::__1::cerr$
race:^std::cout$
race:^std::cerr$
